          "In 1443 King Sejong (세종대왕, r. 1418–1450), the fourth king of the Joseon dynasty, completed a new script for Korean; three years later, in 1446, it was published as the <b>훈민정음</b> (訓民正音). The famous preface, written in Classical Chinese, begins: 國之語音 異乎中國 與文字不相流通 — <i>the speech of our country differs from that of China and does not match the [Chinese] characters</i>. Sejong goes on to say that ordinary people who wished to express themselves could not, and that he had therefore made twenty-eight new letters, wishing only that everyone might learn them easily and use them conveniently every day.",
          "The claim that they could be learned easily was not marketing. A companion volume of 1446, the <b>훈민정음 해례본</b> (訓民正音解例本, <i>Hunminjeongeum Haerye</i>, rediscovered in 1940 and now a UNESCO Memory of the World document), explains the design principle, and it is startlingly modern. The five basic consonants are diagrams of the vocal tract: <b>ㄱ</b> pictures the back of the tongue blocking the throat, <b>ㄴ</b> the tongue tip touching the ridge behind the teeth, <b>ㅁ</b> the outline of the lips, <b>ㅅ</b> the shape of a tooth, and <b>ㅇ</b> the round opening of the throat.",
