          "Korean dictionaries do not use anything resembling ABC order. They use <b>가나다순</b>, which sorts first by the onset consonant in the order ㄱ ㄲ ㄴ ㄷ ㄸ ㄹ ㅁ ㅂ ㅃ ㅅ ㅆ ㅇ ㅈ ㅉ ㅊ ㅋ ㅌ ㅍ ㅎ, then by vowel in the order ㅏ ㅐ ㅑ ㅒ ㅓ ㅔ ㅕ ㅖ ㅗ ㅘ ㅙ ㅚ ㅛ ㅜ ㅝ ㅞ ㅟ ㅠ ㅡ ㅢ ㅣ, then by batchim. North Korea uses a slightly different order (ㅇ is placed last among onsets, and the doubled letters come after all the simple ones), one of several small standardisation differences between the two states. Unicode's Hangul Syllables block is laid out in exactly the South Korean order, so a plain code-point sort of Korean text produces correct dictionary order for free.",
          "The standard Korean keyboard, <b>두벌식</b> (dubeolsik, <i>two-set</i>), puts every consonant on the left hand and every vowel on the right, so typing Korean alternates hands almost every keystroke — a genuinely ergonomic accident of the alphabet's structure. The input method assembles your keystrokes into blocks in real time, which is why a half-typed Korean word briefly shows an incomplete block. Tense consonants are typed with Shift. On phones, the dominant Korean layout is <b>천지인</b> (Cheonjiin), which gives you just three vowel keys — the dot, the horizontal line and the vertical line of the original 1446 design — and builds every vowel from combinations of them. A five-hundred-year-old cosmological design turns out to be an excellent compressed keyboard.",
          "South Korea celebrates <b>한글날</b> on 9 October, the anniversary of the 1446 publication of the 훈민정음; it was restored as a full public holiday in 2013 after decades as a mere commemorative day. Seoul's Gwanghwamun Square has a large seated statue of King Sejong facing down the avenue toward Gyeongbokgung Palace, with a museum devoted to his reign underneath it. The 해례본 manuscript itself, the Kansong copy, is National Treasure No. 70 and was inscribed on the UNESCO Memory of the World register in 1997; UNESCO also names its literacy prize after King Sejong.",
          "On romanisation: you will meet two systems. <b>Revised Romanization</b> (2000), used in this course and on all current South Korean road signs, writes 부산 as <i>Busan</i> and 김치 as <i>gimchi</i>. The older <b>McCune-Reischauer</b> system, still standard in North Korea and in much academic writing, writes <i>Pusan</i> and <i>kimch'i</i> using apostrophes and breves. Personal names are a free-for-all — 이 is officially <i>I</i> under Revised Romanization but essentially everyone writes <i>Lee</i>, and 박 is <i>Bak</i> officially but <i>Park</i> in practice. Treat romanisation as a crutch: it exists to get you through this foundation unit, and the sooner you read the blocks directly, the better your pronunciation will be."
